# Keep the build context small and reproducible — the image restores/publishes from source.
bin/
obj/

# The front-end templates' dependencies and build output. The image runs its own `npm ci`, and
# `COPY . .` happens AFTER it — so a developer's host node_modules would land on top of the
# container's, replacing linux binaries (esbuild, rollup, @tailwindcss/oxide) with darwin or
# windows ones. The publish then fails inside the image with a platform mismatch, or ships a
# tree that cannot run.
**/node_modules/
**/.output/
**/.next/
**/.nuxt/
**/.svelte-kit/
**/dist/

# Local configuration, and the reason this matters more than it looks: the Web SDK copies every
# appsettings*.json into the publish output, and the build stage above runs `COPY . .` before it.
# Without these two lines a developer's appsettings.Development.json — which for a push app holds
# the VAPID PRIVATE KEY `rask new` generated — is published into /app and then carried into the
# final image by `COPY --from=build`. It is never read there (the container runs Production), but a
# signing key has no business inside an image that gets pushed to a registry. Gitignoring the file
# does not cover this: .dockerignore is a separate list, and Docker never reads .gitignore.
appsettings.Development.json
appsettings.Local.json

# Secrets and data that must never enter an image: the env files `rask deploy --env-file` reads, SQLite
# databases and their WAL, `rask db backup` archives, and the folders the batteries write beside the app.
.env
.env.*
*.db
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
*.tgz
mail-pickup/
snapshots/
storage/

.git/
.gitignore
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
*.user
**/.DS_Store
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
